        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 190,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Let the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ be represented as $\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}$. We know that $\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$. This gives us two equations:\n\\[ \\begin{cases} 2a - b = 9 \\\\ 2c - d = 3 \\end{cases} \\]\nand\n\\[ \\begin{cases} a - 3b = 7 \\\\ c - 3d = -1 \\end{cases} \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 747,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 748,
        "char_start": 450,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 322,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 323,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Solve the first system for $a$ and $b$:\n\\[ 2a - b = 9 \\]\n\\[ a - 3b = 7 \\]\nMultiply the second equation by 2:\n\\[ 2a - 6b = 14 \\]\nSubtract the first equation from this result:\n\\[ -5b = 5 \\implies b = -1 \\]\nSubstitute $b = -1$ into $2a - b = 9$:\n\\[ 2a + 1 = 9 \\implies 2a = 8 \\implies a = 4 \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1048,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1049,
        "char_start": 750,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 454,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 455,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Solve the second system for $c$ and $d$:\n\\[ 2c - d = 3 \\]\n\\[ c - 3d = -1 \\]\nMultiply the second equation by 2:\n\\[ 2c - 6d = -2 \\]\nSubtract the first equation from this result:\n\\[ -5d = -5 \\implies d = 1 \\]\nSubstitute $d = 1$ into $2c - d = 3$:\n\\[ 2c - 1 = 3 \\implies 2c = 4 \\implies c = 2 \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1611,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1612,
        "char_start": 1051,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 702,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 703,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: The matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$. To find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}$, we need to transform a general point $(x, y)$ on the line using $\\mathbf{M}$. A point on the line can be written as $(x, 2x + 1)$. Applying $\\mathbf{M}$:\n\\[ \\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1922,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1923,
        "char_start": 1614,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 857,
        "near_end": true,
        "prefix_token_count": 858,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: The image of the point $(x, 2x + 1)$ is $(2x - 1, 4x + 1)$. To express this in the form $y = mx + b$, let $X = 2x - 1$. Then $x = \\frac{X + 1}{2}$. Substitute into the second component:\n\\[ Y = 4x + 1 = 4 \\left( \\frac{X + 1}{2} \\right) + 1 = 2(X + 1) + 1 = 2X + 3 \\]\nThus, $Y = 2X + 3$ or $y = 2x + 3$."
      }
